            <ArticleTitle>Ochronosis of the aortic valve and aorta.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>730-2</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>A 64-year-old male patient with unknown alkaptonuria and severe aortic stenosis and ischemic heart disease was admitted to the authors' institution for elective surgery. The patient underwent aortic valve replacement with a 25-mm aortic valve (ATS Medical, Inc.) and single venous aortocoronary artery bypass grafting for a right coronary artery. Aortotomy revealed typical ochronotic pigmentation of a severely calcified aortic valve and aortic intima. A diagnosis of alkaptonuria was confirmed by evidence of homogentisic acid in the patient's urine, together with histopathological analysis.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
